{"retractions" [[17592186354860 :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$"] [17592186354860 :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-1 & ={1}^{2}+C \\\\ 0-1 & =1+C \\\\ C & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $(\\frac{1}{xy}-\\frac{1}{x})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-y={x}^{2}-2\\,.\n$$"] [17592186354860 :question/answer-fields 17592186354861]] "immutable_answers" [17592186354862 17592186354863 17592186354864 17592186354865 17592186354866] "immutable_fields" [[17592186354861 :answer-field/id] [17592186354861 :answer-field/key] [17592186354861 :answer-field/type] [17592186354861 :answer-field/correct] [17592186354861 :answer-field/choices]]}
